" (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v164/mandlekar22a/mandlekar22a.pdf)\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 13; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v164/mandlekar22a/mandlekar22a.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 595\nL0@P0: What Matters in Learning from Offline Human\nL1@P0: Demonstrations for Robot Manipulation\nL2@P0: Ajay Mandlekar1, Danfei Xu1, Josiah Wong1, Soroush Nasiriany2, Chen Wang1,\nL3@P0: Rohun Kulkarni1, Li Fei-Fei1, Silvio Savarese1, Yuke Zhu2, Roberto Martín-Martín1\nL4@P0: 1Stanford University, 2The University of Texas at Austin\nL5@P0: Abstract: Imitating human demonstrations is a promising approach to endow\nL6@P0: robots with various manipulation capabilities. While recent advances have been\nL7@P0: made in imitation learning and batch (offline) reinforcement learning, a lack of\nL8@P0: open-source human datasets and reproducible learning methods make assessing\nL9@P0: the state of the field difficult. In this paper, we conduct an extensive study of\nL10@P0: six offline learning algorithms for robot manipulation on five simulated and three\nL11@P0: real-world multi-stage manipulation tasks of varying complexity, and with datasets\nL12@P0: of varying quality. Our study analyzes the most critical challenges when learning\nL13@P0: from offline human data for manipulation. Based on the study, we derive a series\nL14@P0: of lessons including the sensitivity to different algorithmic design choices, the\nL15@P0: dependence on the quality of the demonstrations, and the variability based on the\nL16@P0: stopping criteria due to the different objectives in training and evaluation. We\nL17@P0: also highlight opportunities for learning from human datasets, such as the ability\nL18@P0: to learn proficient policies on challenging, multi-stage tasks beyond the scope of\nL19@P0: current reinforcement learning methods, and the ability to easily scale to natural,\nL20@P0: real-world manipulation scenarios where only raw sensory signals are available.\nL21@P0: We have open-sourced our datasets and all algorithm implementations to facilitate\nL22@P0: future research and fair comparisons in learning from human demonstration data at\nL23@P0: https://arise-initiative.github.io/robomimic-web/\nL24@P0: Keywords: Imitation Learning, Offline Reinforcement Learning, Manipulation\nL25@P0: 1 Introduction\nL26@P0: Human supervision has been at the heart of the most significant recent advances in several domains\nL27@P0: such as computer vision [1–4] and natural language processing [5–7]. By intelligently extracting\nL28@P0: information from large-scale human-labeled datasets, autonomous machines have been able to\nL29@P0: reach near- or even super-human performance on decades-old problems such as image recognition\nL30@P0: and question answering. Roboticists have also attempted to tackle robot manipulation through\nL31@P0: learning from human datasets, using the paradigms of Imitation Learning [8–10] and Batch (Offline)\nL32@P0: Reinforcement Learning [11–13], where datasets consisting of robot arm trajectories, action labels at\nL33@P0: each timestep, and possibly reward labels, are used to train closed-loop policies.\nL34@P0: As in other domains, large offline datasets offer several benefits such as scale, portability, and\nL35@P0: reproducible evaluations to measure progress. Recently, there has been considerable progress in\nL36@P0: offline learning for robot manipulation from human demonstrations [9, 14, 10]. Despite these\nL37@P0: advances, the offline learning paradigm has not been nearly as disruptive in robotics as in other\nL38@P0: disciplines – there is a large gap between autonomous robot manipulation capabilities and the wide\nL39@P0: range of tasks that humans can solve effortlessly using physical and cognitive intelligence. What has\nL40@P0: inhibited the use of large human-provided datasets to address this gap?\nL41@P0: In contrast to other domains where supervised learning has been successful, robotic manipulation\nL42@P0: is a time-evolving dynamical system, requiring fine-grained real-time control to guide robot arms\nL43@P0: successfully through tasks – consequently, data collection can present technical challenges requiring\nL44@P0-1: 5th Conference on Robot Learning (CoRL 2021), London, UK.(a) Lift (b) Can (c) Tool Hang (d) Square\nL45@P1: (e) Lift (Real) (f) Can (Real) (g) Tool Hang (Real) (h) Transport\nL46@P1: Figure 1: Tasks. We collect datasets across 6 operators of varying proficiency and evaluate offline policy\nL47@P1: learning methods on 8 challenging manipulation tasks that test a wide range of manipulation capabilities\nL48@P1: including pick-and-place, multi-arm coordination, and high-precision insertion and assembly.\nL49@P1: specialized systems [15], which can explain why large-scale human-provided datasets [16, 17] have\nL50@P1: not been very prevalent. Learning from such datasets can also present several challenges. Human\nL51@P1: demonstrations can differ from machine-generated datasets (a recent trend in benchmarks for offline\nL52@P1: policy learning [18, 19]) due to a non-Markovian decision process, since humans may not act purely\nL53@P1: based on the current observation. There can also be significant variance in both data quality and\nL54@P1: solution strategy when collecting data from multiple humans [20]. Differences from classic supervised\nL55@P1: learning, such as a mismatch between training and evaluation objectives (task success rate), can make\nL56@P1: selecting a final policy challenging [21, 22], especially in real-world settings where evaluating each\nL57@P1: policy on a robot can be infeasible. Finally, offline learning is sensitive to state and action space\nL58@P1: coverage (dataset size) and agent design decisions.\nL59@P1: Studying these challenges in the context of robot manipulation and human-provided datasets could be\nL60@P1: a stepping stone to closing the gap between robot and human manipulation capabilities. Unfortunately,\nL61@P1: a lack of suitable benchmark and human datasets have made studying this setting difficult. Prior\nL62@P1: works are either limited to studying simple 2D environments [23] or using data generated from\nL63@P1: hard-coded policies [24, 25]. In this paper, we address this need by presenting a study of data-driven\nL64@P1: offline policy learning methods on several human-provided robot manipulation datasets. We collect\nL65@P1: task demonstrations from human teleoperators across a broad range of simulated and real world\nL66@P1: manipulation tasks and investigate several factors that play a role in learning from such data.\nL67@P1: From our results, we point out several lessons to guide future research in leveraging human supervision\nL68@P1: for robot manipulation effectively. We find that history-dependent models can be extremely effective\nL69@P1: in learning from single and multi-human datasets while state-of-the-art batch RL algorithms struggle\nL70@P1: to learn from such datasets, and that the choice of observation space and hyperparameters play a\nL71@P1: substantial role in training proficient policies. We also find that there is substantial promise for\nL72@P1: solving more complex tasks using large-scale human datasets and that our insights directly transfer to\nL73@P1: real-world scenarios, making this an important setting to explore further.\nL74@P1: 2 Challenges in Offline Learning from Human Datasets\nL75@P1: In this section, we outline five challenges in offline learning from human datasets that motivate\nL76@P1: different factors that we investigate in our study.\nL77@P1: (C1) Data from Non-Markovian Decision Process. Human demonstrations can differ substantially\nL78@P1: from machine-generated demonstrations because humans may not act purely based on a single current\nL79@P1: observation. External factors (teleoperation device, past actions, history of episode) may all play a\nL80@P1: role. Prior work [20] has noted substantial benefits from leveraging models that are history-dependent\nL81@P1: and / or with temporal abstraction to learn from human demonstrations. We investigate various design\nL82@P1: choices related to such architectures in this study.\nL83@P1-2: 2Dataset BC BC-RNN BCQ CQL HBC IRIS\nL84@P2: Lift (MG) 65.3±2.5 70.7±3.4 91.3±1.9 64.0±2.8 47.3±4.1 96.0±1.6\nL85@P2: Can (MG) 64.7±3.4 68.7±2.5 75.3±0.9 1.3±0.9 40.7±3.4 48.0±6.5\nL86@P2: Lift (PH) 100.0±0.0 100.0±0.0 100.0±0.0 92.7±5.0 100.0±0.0 100.0±0.0\nL87@P2: Can (PH) 95.3±0.9 100.0±0.0 88.7±0.9 38.0±7.5 100.0±0.0 100.0±0.0\nL88@P2: Square (PH) 78.7±1.9 84.0±0.0 50.0±4.95.3±2.5 82.6±0.9 78.7±2.5\nL89@P2: Transport (PH) 17.3±2.5 71.3±6.6 7.3±3.30.0±0.0 48.6±3.8 41.3±3.4\nL90@P2: Tool Hang (PH) 29.3±0.9 19.3±5.00.0±0.00.0±0.0 30.0±7.1 11.3±2.5\nL91@P2: Lift (MH) 100.0±0.0 100.0±0.0 100.0±0.0 56.7±40.3 100.0±0.0 100.0±0.0\nL92@P2: Can (MH) 86.0±4.3 100.0±0.0 62.7±8.2 22.0±5.7 91.3±2.5 92.7±0.9\nL93@P2: Square (MH) 52.7±6.6 78.0±4.3 14.0±4.30.7±0.9 60.7±5.0 52.7±5.0\nL94@P2: Transport (MH) 11.3±2.5 65.3±7.4 2.6±0.90.0±0.0 14.0±1.6 10.7±0.9\nL95@P2: Table 1: Results on Low-Dimensional Observations. We present success rates averaged over 3 seeds for\nL96@P2: each method across the low-dim Machine-Generated (MG), Proficient-Human (PH), and Multi-Human (MH)\nL97@P2: datasets. The results show that methods that model temporal correlations (BC-RNN, HBC, IRIS) exhibit\nL98@P2: strong performance on human datasets. Furthermore, while Batch RL algorithms like BCQ are proficient on\nL99@P2: machine-generated data, they perform poorly on human datasets.\nL100@P2: (C2) Variance in Demonstration Quality from Multiple Humans. Prior work [20, 17] has found\nL101@P2: that data collected from several humans can differ substantially in both demonstration proficiency\nL102@P2: and solution strategy. Differences in supervisor proficiency can manifest in many ways, such as\nL103@P2: large variations in trajectory length and noise in robot movement or mistakes (e.g. missed grasps).\nL104@P2: In our study, we evaluate offline policy learning algorithms on such datasets. While recent batch\nL105@P2: RL algorithms have shown an excellent ability to learn from mixed quality machine-generated\nL106@P2: datasets [26, 27], we empirically find that they fail to learn well from mixed quality human data.\nL107@P2: (C3) Dependence on Dataset Size. Offline policy learning is sensitive to the state and action space\nL108@P2: coverage in the dataset, and by extension, the size of the dataset itself. In our study, we investigate\nL109@P2: how dataset sizes affect policy performance. This analysis is useful to understand the value of adding\nL110@P2: more data – an important consideration since collecting human demonstrations can be costly.\nL111@P2: (C4) Mismatch between Training and Evaluation Objectives. Unlike traditional supervised learn\u0002ing, where model selection can be achieved by using the model with the lowest validation loss [21],\nL112@P2: offline policy learning often suffers from the fact that the training objective is only a surrogate for the\nL113@P2: true objective of interest (e.g. task success rate), and policy performance can change significantly\nL114@P2: from epoch to epoch. This makes it difficult to select the best trained model [19, 28, 29]. In our\nL115@P2: study, we evaluate each policy checkpoint online in the environment in simulation, and report the best\nL116@P2: policy success rate per training run. We use these ground-truth values to understand the effectiveness\nL117@P2: of different selection criteria, and confirm that offline policy selection is an important problem,\nL118@P2: especially in real-world scenarios where large-scale empirical evaluation is difficult.\nL119@P2: (C5) High Sensitivity to Agent Design Decisions. Prior studies on machine-generated datasets have\nL120@P2: shown that offline policy learning can be extremely sensitive to hyperparameter choices [19, 28]. In\nL121@P2: our study, we explore how agent design decisions affect policy performances, including the choice of\nL122@P2: agent architecture, agent observation space, and hyperparameter choices per algorithm. This results\nL123@P2: in several practical conclusions that should prove useful to researchers and practitioners alike. We\nL124@P2: further show that important design decisions made through our study in simulation directly translate\nL125@P2: to effective policy learning on real world tasks and datasets.\nL126@P2: 3 Study Design\nL127@P2: 3.1 Tasks\nL128@P2: We conducted our study across 5 simulated and 3 real world tasks. The tasks were chosen to test a\nL129@P2: broad range of manipulation capabilities. See Fig 1 and Appendix E for more details.\nL130@P2: Lift (sim + real)). The robot arm must lift a small cube. This is the simplest task.\nL131@P2: Can (sim + real). The robot must place a coke can from a large bin into a smaller target bin. Slightly\nL132@P2: more challenging than Lift, since picking the can is harder than picking the cube, and the can must\nL133@P2: also be placed into the bin.\nL134@P2: Square (sim). The robot must pick a square nut and place it on a rod. Substantially more difficult\nL135@P2: than Lift and Pick Place Can due to the precision needed to pick up the nut and insert it on the rod.\nL136@P2-3: 3Dataset BC BC-RNN BCQ CQL HBC IRIS\nL137@P3: Can-Worse 56.7±2.5 92.0±1.6 29.3±10.94.0±3.3 78.7±3.4 77.3±1.9\nL138@P3: Can-Okay 72.0±2.8 95.3±1.9 58.0±8.6 22.0±4.3 97.3±0.9 96.0±0.0\nL139@P3: Can-Better 83.3±2.5 99.3±0.9 62.0±5.9 20.7±7.4 96.7±0.9 96.0±0.0\nL140@P3: Can-Worse-Okay 74.7±5.7 98.7±1.9 50.7±3.8 18.7±2.5 88.0±1.6 87.3±1.9\nL141@P3: Can-Worse-Better 76.0±4.3 100.0±0.0 48.0±4.9 20.7±5.7 90.0±1.6 91.3±2.5\nL142@P3: Can-Okay-Better 90.7±1.9 100.0±0.0 68.7±2.5 30.7±7.7 99.3±0.9 98.0±1.6\nL143@P3: Square-Worse 22.0±4.3 39.3±3.85.3±1.90.0±0.0 44.7±6.8 38.7±0.9\nL144@P3: Square-Okay 27.3±3.4 45.3±2.56.7±1.90.0±0.0 52.0±2.8 42.0±3.3\nL145@P3: Square-Better 58.7±2.5 66.0±2.8 32.0±4.30.7±0.9 61.3±1.9 60.0±1.6\nL146@P3: Square-Worse-Okay 28.7±2.5 55.3±0.9 8.7±1.92.7±1.9 50.7±4.1 43.3±2.5\nL147@P3: Square-Worse-Better 46.7±5.7 73.3±6.2 15.3±2.51.3±0.9 65.3±3.4 56.7±3.4\nL148@P3: Square-Okay-Better 56.7±4.1 74.0±2.8 22.0±4.31.3±0.9 63.3±4.1 56.7±3.8\nL149@P3: Can-Paired 64.0±9.1 70.0±4.3 44.7±1.96.0±1.6 70.7±5.2 75.3±1.9\nL150@P3: Table 2: Results on Suboptimal Human Data. We present success rates averaged over 3 seeds for each method\nL151@P3: across different subsets of the Multi-Human datasets, corresponding to mixtures of demonstrations from “Better”,\nL152@P3: “Adequate”, and “Worse” human operators, and finally on a diagnostic dataset with paired success and failure\nL153@P3: human trajectories for each starting initialization. Results indicate that BC-RNN is a strong baseline, and that\nL154@P3: Batch RL methods perform poorly across all datasets, even on the simple diagnostic dataset.\nL155@P3: Transport (sim). Two robot arms must transfer a hammer from a closed container on a shelf to a\nL156@P3: target bin on another shelf. One robot arm must retrieve the hammer from the container, while the\nL157@P3: other arm must clear the target bin by moving a piece of trash to the nearby receptacle. Finally, one\nL158@P3: arm must hand the hammer over to the other, which must place the hammer in the target bin.\nL159@P3: Tool Hang (sim + real). A robot arm must assemble a frame consisting of a base piece and hook\nL160@P3: piece by inserting the hook into the base, and hang a wrench on the hook. This is the most difficult\nL161@P3: task due to the multiple stages that each require precise, and dexterous, rotation-heavy movements.\nL162@P3: 3.2 Data Collection\nL163@P3: To study the effect of dataset source, we collected data from three different sources – Machine\u0002Generated, Proficient-Human, and Multi-Human (more details in Appendix B).\nL164@P3: Machine-Generated (MG). We collected these datasets by first training a state-of-the-art RL al\u0002gorithm [30] on the Lift and Can task, taking agent checkpoints that are saved regularly during\nL165@P3: training, and collecting 300 rollout trajectories from each checkpoint. Consequently, these datasets\nL166@P3: are comprised of mixtures of expert and suboptimal data, and resemble datasets from common offline\nL167@P3: RL benchmarks [18, 19]. We excluded other tasks because they could not be solved by the RL\nL168@P3: algorithm even with substantial tuning. See the appendix for more details.\nL169@P3: Proficient-Human (PH) and Multi-Human (MH). Datasets are collected by humans through Robo\u0002Turk [15, 17], a remote teleoperation platform. The PH datasets consist of 200 demonstrations\nL170@P3: collected by a single, experienced teleoperator, while the MH datasets consist of 300 demonstrations,\nL171@P3: collected by 6 teleoperators of varying proficiency, each of which provided 50 demonstrations. The 6\nL172@P3: teleoperators consisted of a “better” group of 2 experienced operators, an “okay” group of 2 adequate\nL173@P3: operators, and a “worse” group of 2 inexperienced operators. These data subsets in the Multi-Human\nL174@P3: data allowed us to investigate the ability of algorithms to deal with mixed quality human data.\nL175@P3: Observation Modalities. To study the effect of observation modalities, we capture a diverse set of\nL176@P3: sensor streams when collecting the dataset, including end-effector, gripper fingers, and joints, ground\u0002truth object poses, and images from an external camera and wrist-mounted camera per robot arm (see\nL177@P3: Appendix E). We have two observation spaces – “low-dim” and “image”. Both include end-effector\nL178@P3: poses and gripper finger positions, and only differ in whether ground-truth object information is used\nL179@P3: (low-dim) or whether that information is replaced by the available camera observations (image).\nL180@P3: 3.3 Training and Evaluation Protocols\nL181@P3: There are several approaches to offline imitation learning [31–34, 9, 10, 25, 35, 36] and offline\nL182@P3: reinforcement learning [26, 27, 37–43] (see Appendix A for more discussion on related work). We\nL183@P3: chose to evaluate 6 algorithms in this study – Behavioral Cloning (BC), BC with an RNN policy\nL184@P3: (BC-RNN), Hierarchical Behavioral Cloning (HBC) [10], Batch-Constrained Q-Learning (BCQ) [26],\nL185@P3: Conservative Q-Learning (CQL) [27], and IRIS [20]. BC-RNN, HBC, and IRIS have all been used\nL186@P3-4: 4(a) Observation Space (b) Low-Dim Hyperparameters (c) Image Hyperparameters\nL187@P4: Figure 2: Effect of Observation Space and Hyperparameter Choice. We show how the success rate that\nL188@P4: BC-RNN obtains can drop drastically due to changes to the observation space and hyperparameter settings.\nL189@P4: (a) Low-Dim (b) Image\nL190@P4: Figure 3: Effect of Dataset Size. We study how the BC-RNN success rate changes when lowering the quantity\nL191@P4: of data to 20% and 50%. Results show that less complex tasks (Lift, Can) be learned with a fraction of the data,\nL192@P4: while more complex tasks might benefit from even larger human datasets.\nL193@P4: in prior work to learn offline from teleoperated human demonstrations, while BCQ and CQL are\nL194@P4: commonly-used offline RL algorithms (see Appendix C). We use binary task completion rewards for\nL195@P4: all our experiments. Each agent is trained for N epochs, where each epoch consists of M gradient\nL196@P4: steps, and evaluated every E epochs, by running 50 rollouts in the environment and reporting the\nL197@P4: success rate over a maximum horizon. For each agent, we report the maximum success rate over the\nL198@P4: coarse of training, and average over 3 seeds. For low-dim agents, N = 2000, M = 100, and E = 50,\nL199@P4: and for image agents, N = 600, M = 500, and E = 20 (see Appendix B.2).\nL200@P4: 4 Experiments\nL201@P4: In this section, we present each factor that we explored in our study, and note the relevant challenges\nL202@P4: from Sec. 2 that each pertains to.\nL203@P4: 4.1 Algorithm Comparison on Single and Multi-Human Demonstrations (C1, C2)\nL204@P4: We trained and evaluated all algorithms on the Proficient-Human (PH) and Multi-Human (MH)\nL205@P4: datasets and report the average success rates across 3 seeds in Table 1.\nL206@P4: Observation history is crucial for good performance. There is a substantial performance gap\nL207@P4: between BC-RNN and BC, which highlights the benefits of history-dependence. The performance\nL208@P4: gap is larger for longer-horizon tasks (e.g. ⇠ 55% for Transport (PH) compared to ⇠ 5% for Square\nL209@P4: (PH)) and for multi-human data compared to single-human data (e.g. ⇠ 25% for Square (MH)\nL210@P4: compared to ⇠ 5% for Square (PH)). Interestingly, results are lower for MH datasets compared to PH\nL211@P4: datasets, even though the MH datasets contain 100 more demos (300 demos vs. 200 demos). This\nL212@P4: most likely stems from the presence of suboptimal and multimodal data in the MH datasets.\nL213@P4: Batch RL algorithms perform poorly on Human Datasets. Recent batch (offline) RL algorithms\nL214@P4: such as BCQ and CQL have demonstrated excellent results in learning from suboptimal and multi\u0002modal agent-generated datasets. Our results confirm the capacity of such algorithms to work well –\nL215@P4: BCQ in particular performs strongly on our agent-generated MG datasets that consist of a diverse\nL216@P4: mixture of good and poor policies. Surprisingly though, neither BCQ nor CQL performs particularly\nL217@P4: well on these human-generated datasets. This puts the ability of such algorithms to learn from more\nL218@P4-5: 5(a) Effect of Policy Selection Criteria\nL219@P5: Dataset BC BC-RNN BCQ CQL\nL220@P5: Lift (PH) 100.0±0.0 100.0±0.0 98.0±1.6 52.0±13.0\nL221@P5: Can (PH) 97.3±1.9 98.0±0.9 86.7±2.50.7±0.9\nL222@P5: Square (PH) 62.0±4.9 82.0±0.0 41.3±4.1-\nL223@P5: Transport (PH) 55.3±6.2 72.0±4.3 0.7±0.9-\nL224@P5: Tool Hang (PH) 20.0±5.9 67.3±4.1 3.3±0.9-\nL225@P5: Lift (MH) 100.0±0.0 100.0±0.0 93.3±0.9 11.3±9.3\nL226@P5: Can (MH) 85.3±0.9 96.0±1.6 77.3±6.80.0±0.0\nL227@P5: Square (MH) 46.0±1.6 76.7±3.4 17.3±7.5-\nL228@P5: Transport (MH) 18.7±2.5 42.0±1.6 0.0±0.0-\nL229@P5: Table 3: Results on Image Observations.\nL230@P5: Figure 5: (left) Effect of Policy Selection Criteria. We compare how performance decreases when choosing the\nL231@P5: policy to evaluate by using the lowest validation loss, or when using the final trained checkpoint, with respect to\nL232@P5: the best policy performance. (right) Results on Image Observations. We present success rates for each method\nL233@P5: across the image observation human datasets. BC-RNN maintains nearly the same performance as learning from\nL234@P5: ground-truth observations, providing an optimistic view for learning with real-world raw sensory observations.\nL235@P5: natural dataset distributions into question (instead of those collected via RL exploration or pre-trained\nL236@P5: agents). There is an opportunity for future work in batch RL to resolve this gap.\nL237@P5: 4.2 Learning from Suboptimal Human Data (C2)\nL238@P5: To further investigate how algorithms deal with suboptimal human data, we split our MH datasets\nL239@P5: into smaller subsets based on the proficiency of the human operators. The MH-Better, MH-Okay,\nL240@P5: and MH-Worse are the 100 demo subsets corresponding to the 2 “better”, 2 “okay”, and 2 “worse”\nL241@P5: operators respectively, while MH Worse-Okay, MH Worse-Better, and MH Okay-Better are the 200\nL242@P5: demo subsets corresponding to the mixture of the previous subsets. Similar data mixtures have been\nL243@P5: used for evaluations in batch RL [18]. Appendix B shows the average trajectory lengths in each data\nL244@P5: subset – lower quality datasets contain demonstrations that take more time to solve the task.\nL245@P5: BC-RNN is a strong baseline on suboptimal human data, but there is room for improvement.\nL246@P5: Table 2 shows that BC exhibits a large performance gap between the Better and Worse 100-demo\nL247@P5: subsets (roughly 27% and 35% for Can and Square respectively). Interestingly, BC-RNN is able\nL248@P5: to nearly eliminate this gap in performance on the Can task, but not on the Square task. However,\nL249@P5: BC-RNN outperforms BC on all datasets (7%-35% improvement). Comparing results on the 100\nL250@P5: Better demonstrations and 100 Okay demonstrations to the 200 Worse-Better demonstrations and 200\nL251@P5: Worse-Okay demonstrations further allows us to analyze how adding 100 “worse” demonstrations\nL252@P5: impacts the performance of each algorithm. Most algorithms decline in performance while BC-RNN\nL253@P5: is able to uniformly improve from the added data. Comparing the performance of BC-RNN on the\nL254@P5: 200-demo Square mixture datasets (55.3%, 73.3%, 74.0%) to the high-quality 200-demo Square (PH)\nL255@P5: dataset (84.0%) shows that there is still room for algorithms to improve on the use of this data.\nL256@P5: Diagnostic dataset shows that Batch RL struggles in simpler settings as well. The final row of\nL257@P5: Table 2 shows additional results on a diagnostic dataset termed Can-Paired, where a single operator\nL258@P5: collected 2 demonstrations for each of 100 task initializations – one successful demonstration, and\nL259@P5: one where the can is tossed outside of the bin (task failure), for a total of 200 demonstrations. There\nL260@P5: is a strong expectation for batch RL algorithms to be able to distinguish between actions leading\nL261@P5: to successful placement and actions leading to task failure, but even in this simple setting, most\nL262@P5: algorithms suffer, providing a pessimistic view of the state-of-the-art. The 5% improvement that IRIS\nL263@P5: provides over BC-RNN suggests that introducing history-dependence into state-of-the-art batch RL\nL264@P5: algorithms might be a promising direction for future work.\nL265@P5: 4.3 Effect of Observation Space (C5)\nL266@P5: Learning from image observations can match low-dim agent performance. In Table 3, we\nL267@P5: present policy learning results when using image observations instead of ground-truth object locations\nL268@P5: – an important setting for real-world policy learning. BC-RNN still maintains superior performance\nL269@P5: improvements over BC on the complex Square and Transport tasks, and with the exception of\nL270@P5: Transport (MH), maintains nearly the same performance as learning from ground-truth observations.\nL271@P5: This result provides an optimistic view for learning with real-world raw sensory observations.\nL272@P5-6: 6Features used for robot proprioception can matter. In Fig 2a, we study the effect of adding end ef\u0002fector velocities to the observations (+ EEF Vel), and joint positions and velocities to the observations\nL273@P6: (+ Joint). Surprisingly, we find that including end effector velocity information, and joint information\nL274@P6: hurts agents trained on low-dim observations substantially (49%-88% relative performance drop),\nL275@P6: while image-based agents are more tolerant to the inclusion of this extra information (2%-29%\nL276@P6: relative performance drop). We hypothesize that performance drops might be due to overfitting\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2019/file/947018640bf36a2bb609d3557a285329-Paper.pdf)\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2019/file/947018640bf36a2bb609d3557a285329-Paper.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 733\n"
